{
	title: 'Find ranks tabs by evidence; in: scopes and quoted phrases',
	spec: `Find answers "which tab did this?": typing compact surfaces the tab that implemented COMPACT, not the many tabs that merely used it. Without a single-tab scope, results are one row per tab, grouped before the result limit (top 50 tabs). A row shows the tab's strongest evidence as its snippet, a lowercase why label naming that evidence, and its match count; Enter or a click opens the tab at that evidence. Expanding a tab (Right in the results) sets the query's scope to in:<id>, which lists that tab's matching cards one per row, best first. Terminal and web share this behavior; only visuals the terminal cannot draw differ.

Evidence, strongest first: current title, former title (a /rename in its history), a commit made in that tab whose subject matches, a user request (text the user typed, the match within its first 400 characters), a commit matching only its file list, a reply, pasted text later in a user message, thinking, tool calls and output, everything else (notices, inbox, command output). A copied commit notice ("this session did not change") is not authorship. A fork's history before its creation belongs to its origin and is skipped in the fork. A tab's score is its best evidence weight times match quality (word boundaries, phrase), plus a small bonus for each kind of strong evidence (title, former title, commit, request) and for their count. Recency only breaks ties between scores that round alike. No LLM calls or embeddings.

Query syntax, parsed in common/find.ts: words match anywhere (word starts rank higher); "quoted words" match only as a whole-word phrase; -word excludes evidence whose tab id, tab name or text contains it; since:30m|12h|3d|2w keeps evidence newer than that, still ranked by relevance; in:<scope> narrows, comma-separated; tab values union, kind values union, and the two intersect: here (the tab Find opened from), a session id (181-ibm), a tab number or range (10-19), . (tabs whose cwd is the current tab's cwd), a path (~/sync/x: tabs whose cwd is it or under it), and the kind names user, hal, reasoning, tools, commits, other. Kind names are reserved; an unknown value is an error naming the accepted forms. model: and cwd: no longer exist.

The filter toggles are user, Hal, reasoning, tools, commits, other (user requests; Hal's replies; thinking; tool calls and output; commits; everything else), all on by default; in:<kind> narrows the same way. The dialog's first lines read "Find anything: tab names, requests, commits, tool output" and "Examples: compact in:here · "live file" · rebase -conflict · since:24h · /help find for more". /help find documents every form above, in:here first.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'The title carries the status (Find · 50 tabs); a failure alone gets its own red row. Batches arrive within milliseconds, so the title keeps its last settled text until a search ends; only the slow first index scan shows progress (user: never show something for 50 ms).',
		'Load more: the worker keeps each channel ranking; a repeated find request with a higher limit resends it without searching again. Terminal asks when the selection is within 10 rows of the end, the web also when the list scrolls within half a view. Find drops the 12/50 position.',
		'Card rows key by session, block and kind: a tool call and its output share a block id, and keying by block alone hid one of them.',
		'Rows show the tab current cwd (session.ason, reloaded after each history catch-up so /cd shows); a cwd match is evidence ranked like the title.'
	],
	needs: ['6m'],
	foldInto: ['6m']
}
